WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.130
PABLO CAIF: Good afternoon.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.610
Thanks for coming
to my presentation.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:27.490
This presentation is about
a story of an Australian ISP

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:32.000
and how they were trying to
deal with their big data,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.190
and how we, with
a very small team,

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.860
were able, by using
BigQuery and Dataflow,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:46.350
to help them to finally get
useful insights out of it.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.040
So I'm going to tell you a
little bit more about myself

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.560
first.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.230
So I didn't mention before,
my name is Pablo Caif.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.140
I'm a software engineer.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.470
I've been in the industry
a bit longer than 13 years.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.860
And I work for
Shine Technologies.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.880
Shine Technologies is
a consultancy company

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.320
based in Melbourne,
Australia, or Down

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.690
Under as you call it here.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.510
And we basically help customers.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:23.640
And one of our customers,
the customer of this story,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.070
is Telstra.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.830
I'm also a Google
Developer expert

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.630
for the Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.030
And although I come
from Australia,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.530
I'm sorry to tell
you it's not going

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.250
to be an Australian accent
here, because I'm originally

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.290
from Argentina.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.540
So plenty of
Spanish accent here.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.490
So let's talk a little
bit about Telstra.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:58.060
So Telstra, it's one of the
largest ISP and mobile operator

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.660
in Australia.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.910
They own most of
the mobile network

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:07.170
and the actual telephone
network in Australia.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.030
And they provide services
like home internet

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:17.510
access, mobile access, and
broadband access as well.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:21.500
And they have a lot of
accounts, customers.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.790
Between home internet access
and mobile access and stuff

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.830
like that, they have more
than 20 million accounts.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.630
And if we take into
account that people

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.640
in Australia, the population
is just 24 million,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.150
that's a fair share.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.480
They're a very large company.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.700
They have more than
32,000 employees.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.210
And they make a lot of money.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.790
But there is something
particular about them,

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:03.565
which is that they have
a lot of data to process.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.220
So all of the stuff that
I'm going to tell you

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.970
is actually about
a smaller business

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:17.110
unit of Telstra, which
is Telstra Media.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:21.910
And they are a big
digital publisher.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.890
And what they do is they
own several very high

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.680
profile websites in Australia.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.160
And as many as you
might now, in Australia,

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.600
we like sports quite a lot.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.832
So two of these websites are
the AFL, the Australian Football

00:03:41.832 --> 00:03:47.320
League, and the NRL, the
National Rugby League.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.860
And these websites, especially
when they're in season

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.082
and games are being played
and stuff like that,

00:03:55.082 --> 00:03:57.927
you can imagine people are
constantly checking the results

00:03:57.927 --> 00:03:58.760
and stuff like that.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.080
So they get a lot of traffic.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.300
So around 5,000 requests per
second, which is quite a lot.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.730
And also if you would
imagine the websites,

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.470
they have the central space
with all the information,

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.899
stuff the would be of interest.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.620
But they also have
digital real estate, space

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.710
that they take up advantage of.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.210
So what do they do
with this space?

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.740
They put lots and lots of ads.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:45.990
So what they do with
these ads is basically

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.420
use targeted advertising
to generate revenue.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.210
And this targeted
advertising, it's

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:59.280
basically through
basic demographics,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.365
which means that we have
several data points.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.300
The way they are
able to serve the ads

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:12.070
is using DoubleClick
for Publishers, or DFP,

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:12.920
as an ad server.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.620
And every time an
ad is being shown,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.810
that's what we
call an impression.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.210
Or someone clicks on an ad,
that we get a log entry.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.530
And that log entry contains a
lot of very interesting data

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:30.970
points that we can use.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.940
And they make a lot of
actually information out of it.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:43.740
So imagine, 15,000
requests per second

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.540
hitting our page, our web pages.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.760
If we were to try to
collect all this stuff,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.440
it would turn into 5 billion
impressions per month,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.205
or something around
three terabytes per month

00:05:56.205 --> 00:05:58.020
of information.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.170
Just to put it in perspective,
the age of our sun

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.740
is 4.5 billion years.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:13.790
So to put everything
into context,

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:20.350
it was the year 2007,
almost 10 years ago.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:24.250
And our customer Telstra,
they say, why don't we

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.720
start getting some
useful information out

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.210
of all these logs, all this
stuff we keep generating?

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.620
But they found
out that it wasn't

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.660
that easy to do back
at that time using DFP,

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:41.110
because imagine
business people trying

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.084
to generate these reports.

00:06:43.084 --> 00:06:44.000
It was time consuming.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.260
The stuff that we're
trying to find out,

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:54.360
it was very difficult to find
in such a large amount of data.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.480
And whenever they were able
to get anything out of it,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.680
it took them a long time.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.300
And one of the things that they
were finding a bit limiting

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:11.200
was also there wasn't any
out of the box visualizations

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.650
that they could use.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.290
So they call us and they
say, can you help us?

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:23.200
So we'd been working
with them before.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.070
And we decided to
assemble a team.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:32.260
So this business unit of Telstra
is a relatively independent

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.280
business unit.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.840
They have their own budget.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.610
And they can do
certain innovation.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:45.720
So we assembled a team,
just two engineers, one BA,

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.890
and that was all we had.

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:55.690
So we said OK, how we
can tackle this problem?

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:59.050
So we tried to
analyze all this data.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:04.720
And what we did, what anyone
would do back in 2007,

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.410
tried to put it
in data warehouse,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:12.040
in Oracle instances
using servers,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.130
like renting our own servers,
rack and stack infrastructure,

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.650
which a lot of headaches,
of course, because it wasn't

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:22.340
really easy to get a-- every
time we wanted a new server,

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.610
it could take six months to get
it approved, and then another

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:28.000
maybe three months
until we finally

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.320
would get the actual server.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.054
And then configuration
and stuff like that, which

00:08:32.054 --> 00:08:33.220
takes time and is expensive.

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:41.220
The amount of data was
such a huge amount of data

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:46.240
that we were only able to
look and analyze just the 10%.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.580
Imagine trying to run
queries on an Oracle instance

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:54.780
like data warehouse
and stuff like that.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:58.830
And it turned out that it was
very costly for this business

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.320
unit.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:02.070
And one of the major
problems they had

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.410
is every time we were
getting more data,

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:07.210
it was hard to scale.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:10.510
As I said before,
getting a new sever,

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.600
waiting six to eight months
to get it actually in,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.860
and even paying for some
stuff that we might not

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.230
be using all the time.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:28.560
So at a very high level, this
is how our solution looked like.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.485
So we had the request
come into our web pages.

00:09:35.668 --> 00:09:37.320
And we were using,
as I mentioned

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.720
before, Double Click for
Publishers to serve the ads.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:44.840
And we were importing
all these logs

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.005
and trying to put them
in these data warehouses.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:53.860
But things didn't really
work as we expected.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.760
We ended up a little
bit like this,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.640
putting out fires all the time.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:04.390
So you can see the two
engineers and the BA.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:09.640
So things like running out
of space on the database,

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:15.020
having to contact DBAs
to resize tablespaces,

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:18.790
having hard drives breaking
down and someone calling us

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.650
and telling us, look,
your old data, you

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.755
are not going to be able to use
all this data until probably

00:10:24.755 --> 00:10:27.380
two, three days until we recover
all the backups and everything

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:30.065
is back online.

00:10:30.065 --> 00:10:31.375
It wasn't really easy.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:38.277
So we came to realize that we
were caring too much about all

00:10:38.277 --> 00:10:39.110
this infrastructure.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:43.165
It was a huge [INAUDIBLE]
for us to try to take it

00:10:43.165 --> 00:10:44.380
off all the situations.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:48.970
But we really wanted to get
results out of all this data.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.450
That was all we really cared.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:11:00.130
So back in 2007, we found
ourselves in a dead end.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:05.960
Moving this data from DFP to
our internal infrastructure

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.900
was very difficult and
it took a long time.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.669
We saturated all the networks
because of all the traffic

00:11:14.669 --> 00:11:15.460
we were generating.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.330
So we were constantly getting
calls and people annoyed

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.320
because everything
was running slow.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:28.130
And trying to run queries over
such a huge amount of data,

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:29.700
it was really, really slow.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.710
And I'm not talking
about minutes or hours.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:33.570
It might take days.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:37.180
We might just kick off the
query, come back the next day

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:39.630
and hope that it
will actually finish.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:41.370
Sometimes it might
just crash and we

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.965
would have to contact DBAs so
they could tune the database

00:11:44.965 --> 00:11:45.715
a little bit more.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:53.300
So back in 2007, this part
was really, really big for us.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:57.410
And we didn't really
know what to do.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:02.450
So we found ourselves
drowning in all this data,

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:05.800
but trying desperately to
get insights out of it.

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:13.780
So a long time after that, we
basically put these in a drawer

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:17.650
or on a back burner as
we say in Australia.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:23.410
2012, several
years later, things

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:25.130
have changed quite a lot.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:27.800
So Google was offering
cloud services.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:32.410
And this is what they were
offering back in 2012.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:37.580
And we hear about a few things--
App Engine, Cloud Storage.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:40.890
But we noticed something
very interesting there,

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.590
which was BigQuery.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:46.970
So with the other engineer
we started our research

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:49.210
and saying, well what
is BigQuery thing?

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.590
I'm sure you've heard quite
a lot about BigQuery already.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.335
This is the second day
actually of GTP Next.

00:12:59.335 --> 00:13:02.829
But just going to tell you what
was very interesting for us.

00:13:02.829 --> 00:13:04.120
So it's analytics as a service.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:12.280
And this was a very important
point for us-- no ops.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:16.080
So stop worrying about
infrastructure and problems

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.040
and situations like that.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.290
We didn't need to think
about installing BigQuery

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:24.000
anywhere or setting up
clusters or configuring

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.930
things or anything of that.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.694
Just create an account,
put in the data,

00:13:29.694 --> 00:13:30.610
and querying the data.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:37.300
It was able to scale
to petabytes, which

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:41.280
is what we were looking for.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.860
And it has a SQL interface.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.750
So as I mentioned before,
we were using Oracle.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:48.335
We were familiar with SQL.

00:13:48.335 --> 00:13:49.850
It was perfect for us.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.610
We could just pretty
much use the same syntax

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.680
and get the data out.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.700
And it's really, really fast.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:06.550
So wonder ourselves,
can we actually

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:10.550
use this BigQuery tool
to solve all this problem

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:13.450
we were having back in 2007?

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.960
So we removed the dust
off of the whole problem,

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.900
took it out, and
started thinking.

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:23.500
And we found out
that it was possible,

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.420
there was a way of
solving this problem.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:30.770
So we were able to get all
this logs, ad serving logs

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:37.500
from DoubleClick for Publishers
and put them in cloud storage.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.990
And since with BigQuery
it is very easy

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:43.160
to import data directly
from cloud storage getting

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:48.170
CSV files, we put
everything in BigQuery.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:51.810
And then we
connected to Tableau.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.745
And we were finally able
to get the insights out.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:03.330
So all of these
impossible things

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:08.280
we couldn't achieve before, all
of the sudden became possible.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:12.620
We were able to move the data
extremely fast because we were

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.780
within the same infrastructure.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:18.320
We didn't need to download
that as we were doing before.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:21.800
We stopped worrying so much
about the infrastructure.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:23.300
No more calling DBAs.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:28.340
No more breaking networks or
broke down servers and stuff

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:30.980
like that.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.115
Our costs exponentially reduced.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:39.700
And these long
hours or maybe days

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:42.490
hoping that a query would
finish all of a sudden

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:43.650
turned into seconds.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:46.800
Just imagine seconds to
get your results out.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.890
And this allowed
our customer Telstra

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:57.520
to get extremely fast time
to get their insights out

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.070
of all these logs.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.110
And other very
interesting things

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.280
we found using
BigQuery were it was

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.250
great for doing ad hoc reports.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:15.320
So I remember once a
DBA came to me and said,

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:18.200
I'm about to go to a
very important meeting

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:20.990
with the stakeholders,
and I need

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:25.030
to query around two or
three years worth of data.

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:25.751
Can you do it?

00:16:25.751 --> 00:16:26.250
Yes.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.700
I just sit there,
wrote some SQL.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:29.435
There you go.

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:38.010
As I mentioned before, very
easy to move the data from DFP

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:42.900
to BigQuery because it was all
within Google infrastructure.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.780
And we were using Java.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.580
We are still using Java for
most of the stuff we do.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.550
So it was very natural
for us to use the SDK.

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:56.190
So as you might now, BigQuery
has an SDK in Java, Python,

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.040
and a few other languages.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.360
So we're using the Java SDK.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:07.369
And it was really
cost effective for us.

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:08.730
But how cost effective?

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:14.220
So if you just try to
calculate paying for DBAs,

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:19.230
paying for your servers,
paying for people looking

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.450
after the whole
infrastructure, I

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:24.319
don't think you can get it for
any less than, I don't know,

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:26.700
$40, $50k per moth.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:30.980
For us, it ended up
being just $1,500

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.960
per month per 100 terabytes.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:38.160
Preach it.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.910
So I'm going to tell
you a few things

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:47.100
we learned when we were
using BigQuery that I'd

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:48.740
like to share with you.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:55.705
One very interesting thing
we found that was the cache.

00:17:55.705 --> 00:17:58.200
So it was very useful
for us to save money.

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:04.340
So BigQuery allows you to cache
the result of the queries.

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:07.770
So when you're running
a query, if you

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:10.260
display the query
options and take that,

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:15.360
use the cache results, it will
basically cache the results.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:19.530
And next time you run the
query, you won't pay for that.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:22.880
So this here is an example.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:26.530
This query will
process 11.5 gigabytes.

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:29.470
So when it runs, it
takes two seconds.

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:33.040
And as I tell us
before, it actually

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.740
processed 11.5 gigabytes.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.750
But when we run it
the second time,

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:41.926
you can see that the results
just come from the cache.

00:18:41.926 --> 00:18:45.510
So you don't pay to
process that data again.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:53.700
Another very useful thing we
found was the table decorators.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:19:00.590
Table decorators allow you to
get a snapshot of your data.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.780
And it's very
useful for when you

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:04.280
have a table where
you're constantly

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:06.880
streaming information in.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.040
So for example,
this table, if I do

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.320
SELECT *, which is not a
recommended thing to do,

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:14.695
it's going to
process-- I'm going

00:19:14.695 --> 00:19:18.085
to go through 460 gigabytes.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:28.710
And if I put the table decorator
just to restrict the last hour,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:33.155
you see how it gets drastically
reduced to 67 megabytes.

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:39.740
Another thing we
found very useful

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:45.910
was the idea of start
with small data sets.

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:49.280
So when you're
writing your queries

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:52.820
and you're trying to figure out
how you want to have your SQLs

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:56.370
and you want to join to several
tables and stuff like that,

00:19:56.370 --> 00:20:01.830
it's always a good idea to
save the data to a small table,

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:07.380
like just maybe 1,000 rows,
100,000 or something like that.

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:09.130
So you can see
here, for example,

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:14.110
if I was to play with this
table, for example, just

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:19.200
doing that would
process 11.5 gigabytes.

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:23.000
However, if I save
only 1,000 records,

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:28.780
the table gets reduced
to 38 kilobytes,

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:30.573
which is completely different.

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:38.300
And this is the the
last step for BigQuery.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:40.190
Another thing we
found very useful

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:43.510
is as we were using
BigQuery, it's very easy

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:45.620
to forget tables along the way.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:50.370
So you play with tables,
you get several gigabytes

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:52.260
worth of data in tables.

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:54.650
So anything is
actually used, the Time

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:59.130
to Live feature, that it can
also be used in data sets.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:02.990
But here's an example on the
table when using the Java SDK.

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:05.220
So just set at expiration time.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:07.690
In this case it's just a minute.

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:11.810
And once you apply the change,
the table will be removed.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:15.690
So it's very good when you're
having temporary tables, again

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:16.910
just set an expiration time.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:24.510
And I just want to reiterate,
just two engineers.

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:25.260
That was the team.

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:32.990
So we decided to move to
the next stage, right?

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:38.010
We had all this stuff going,
getting all the logs from DFP.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:42.970
And we say, why not
try in real time?

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:48.110
We immediately started seeing
a few very interesting things

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:49.220
about it.

00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:53.010
So one thing will
be the campaign

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:57.720
managers would be able to get
very good feedback about how

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:58.930
their ads were going.

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.060
And as a consequence
of that, they

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:08.070
would be able to modify if
maybe they weren't targeting

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:11.130
the right demographics.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.700
They wanted to change
something, they

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.120
were able to change
it on the fly.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:20.230
And that allowed the business
to actually move with it

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:23.620
and make decisions as the
things were actually happening,

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:25.410
not after they happened.

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.130
And this batch
processing we were

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:36.700
doing before, of taking data
out of DFP, it was great.

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:38.440
But it was running every night.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:41.470
And it had a slow
turn around time.

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:48.210
And a very interesting thing
for us was the wow factor.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.660
So imagine seeing a map
of Australia, a heat

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:55.180
map with different
points where you're

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:57.710
getting your impressions
by geolocation

00:22:57.710 --> 00:23:03.554
and distributing the
population or maybe showing

00:23:03.554 --> 00:23:09.570
if it was tablet,
desktop, or mobile phones.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:16.300
So we decided to use
the streaming stuff

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:18.470
that BigQuery has.

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:22.310
So BigQuery has a streaming API.

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:25.430
And the way we implemented
this was creating

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:29.610
a very simple JavaScript
tag that it was basically

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:34.620
just pinging one very
simple sever app.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:39.910
And every time someone
was seeing an ad

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:42.580
or maybe besieging the
website, that was directly

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:43.480
pinging our server.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:48.774
That way impressions
and clicks were directly

00:23:48.774 --> 00:23:49.690
streaming to BigQuery.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:57.530
And in order to being able to
see results much more easily

00:23:57.530 --> 00:24:00.500
and save money, we we're
using table decorator

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:03.920
so we could reduce the threshold
of data we were querying.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:05.800
So maybe just have
the last 10 minutes

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.690
so you could see
much bigger changes.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:15.080
And then we built a
dashboard using D3.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:20.830
D3 is a JavaScript library
for doing graphs and stuff

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:22.870
like that.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:25.100
And this is how
our solution looked

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.110
at a very, very high level.

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:33.130
So as I mentioned before,
we had the requests

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:35.340
hitting our severs.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:40.120
We deploy our app to App Engine.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:44.385
And then we were streaming
every single request

00:24:44.385 --> 00:24:46.510
with all this log information
directly to BigQuery.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:54.432
And here is a demo
of part of the app.

00:24:54.432 --> 00:24:56.375
So let me show you how it works.

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:05.660
So what you can see
there is each dot

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:10.890
is several impressions actually
in that geographic zone,

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.210
geographic area.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:15.250
And you can see
there how we actually

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:21.230
are splitting the whole
amount, the total impressions,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:24.630
by state in Australia.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.360
And we're also getting
an average of impressions

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.030
per minute and splitting
it between mobile, tablet,

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:30.680
and online.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.020
Online will be the desktop.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:37.575
So let's move back
to the slides.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:45.180
Again, just two engineers.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.470
That was the team,
just two of us.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:56.620
OK, so feels like that
went really interesting.

00:25:56.620 --> 00:26:01.890
We were able to, first, analyze
all this amount of data,

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:06.030
process it, and get insight
immediately using BigQuery,

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:10.620
and then have this
streaming thing going on.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:16.950
And the business
started realizing

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.600
how powerful BigQuery was.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:23.370
So they engaged data scientists.

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:28.150
And what they wanted to do
is to alter the distribution

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:33.010
in the sample set to be more
representative of the audience,

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:37.130
so complex statistical things.

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:40.400
We gave them an
[INAUDIBLE] of our data.

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:43.410
And they went away,
did some simulations

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.800
and stuff like that.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:47.740
And they came back with
a statistical model,

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:51.500
very simple, just half
a page, simple maths.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:27:00.390
We got it done in maybe half
an hour, just 20 lines of SQL.

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:02.130
I was there.

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:06.130
And our customers,
Telstra, was really happy.

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:09.530
But they wanted more.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:12.720
So we gave more data
to the data scientists.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:16.320
Data scientists went away,
run their simulations again.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:18.290
And after three or
four months, they

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:23.920
came up with something like
this, like crazy complex thing.

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:29.330
So we were completely
lost, something

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:34.490
like 30 pages of
specifications, operations,

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:37.814
aggregations at several levels.

00:27:37.814 --> 00:27:39.230
The way that this
model worked, it

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:41.130
needed to access
billions of rows

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:44.105
to derive these deeper insights
they wanted to get out.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:52.110
And it became too complex
to be written in SQL.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.810
And one of the things we
were actually trying to do

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:59.460
is being able to
have something that

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:02.660
could go to-- be a
maintainable product,

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:04.590
just raise it to
production and use it.

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:07.240
So we wanted to have unit
tests, write unit tests,

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:12.469
being able to have something
we can work and iterate on it.

00:28:12.469 --> 00:28:14.760
And we started realizing that
we needed kind of the map

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:18.250
reduce style of analysis to
solve these kinds of problems.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:25.650
And that's when
Dataflow came to us.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:30.050
So this is actually
part of a funny story.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.140
As part of all these
interesting adventures

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:36.010
we had with BigQuery, we engaged
part of the Google engineers

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.860
that worked on BigQuery.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:48.150
And this is back 2014,
beginning of 2015.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:52.670
Back then, Dataflow was in
alpha, early alpha access.

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.200
So they invited us to
start testing and playing

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:56.210
with Dataflow.

00:28:56.210 --> 00:28:58.090
And we found that it
was really, really great

00:28:58.090 --> 00:28:59.600
for what we needed it.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.100
Of course now Dataflow
is in production,

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:05.690
generally are viable.

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:11.090
Just again, you probably heard
a lot about Dataflow already.

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:13.500
I'm just going to
quickly tell you

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:15.840
it's massively
parallel and scalable.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:20.550
So you can run complex
operations at scale.

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:23.626
You see it's at a higher
level than MapReduce.

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:30.020
So all the stuff you
basically do with MapReduce,

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:33.110
imagine that you get a
high level of abstraction

00:29:33.110 --> 00:29:36.270
with much more common
operations and you can actually

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:44.160
chain common things
like GROUP BY or maybe

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:48.040
simple transformations or
stuff like that together.

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:50.910
And it has a service
part and an SDK.

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.530
It's great for doing ETL.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:00.370
But it is also
really, really good

00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:03.560
for doing complex analysis.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:08.200
And that's what we really needed
doing this complex-- resolving

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:11.680
this complex problem.

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:16.130
And a very interesting thing
is that it's fully managed.

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:19.900
Again, no worries
about infrastructure.

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:22.960
We didn't need to
install any Hadoop

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:27.500
or install or configure clusters
or do anything sort of in that.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:31.185
So imagine just two developers,
just two software engineers

00:30:31.185 --> 00:30:35.172
who can really try to do that.

00:30:35.172 --> 00:30:38.980
And it's very cost efficient.

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:44.780
So we went and implemented
our model using Dataflow.

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:48.700
And this is how our
solution looked like.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:55.420
So we still had the batch side
of the thing, the solution just

00:30:55.420 --> 00:31:00.430
getting the logs out of DFP,
putting them in BigQuery.

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:04.260
And then taking advantage
of the streaming side,

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:08.700
we got all the
data from BigQuery.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:12.080
We implement our statistical
model using Dataflow

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:13.800
and put all the results
back to BigQuery.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:20.940
And then we were finally
able to get these insights

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:23.500
we were looking for.

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:25.150
Unfortunately I
can't really tell

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:29.650
you too much about this
model because it's under NDA.

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.030
So I'd really like
to tell you more,

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:35.154
but that's the reality of it.

00:31:35.154 --> 00:31:45.590
And in terms of the
costs, imagine a game,

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:49.540
trying to manage big
clusters, having people

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:51.470
helping you with the
infrastructural or maybe

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:54.620
to set up operating system or
installing Hadoop or anything

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:55.270
like that.

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:58.520
You can't really get for
anything lower than probably,

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:01.910
I don't know, $30k or
something per month.

00:32:01.910 --> 00:32:05.240
For us, it was only
$700 per month.

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:07.670
And we were processing
around 50 terabytes.

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:16.714
So let's move to a few Dataflow
tips, things we learned

00:32:16.714 --> 00:32:17.880
when we were using Dataflow.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:25.580
So a few things we found
that it's not a good idea

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:30.450
to try to do, you need
to be aware of long

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:33.360
running operations when you're
writing your transforms.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:37.330
So as you might know,
transforms are actually

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.050
the atomic operations you do.

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:41.040
You get an input data.

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.650
You do something with the data,
and then you output the result.

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:48.136
And you need to be aware that
this will run multiple times.

00:32:48.136 --> 00:32:49.760
And it will have
scaled with your data.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.642
If you have billions
of rows and you're

00:32:51.642 --> 00:32:54.100
trying to read from BigQuery
and you have billions of rows,

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:57.110
you will actually be
doing this billion time.

00:32:57.110 --> 00:32:59.932
So if you are trying to go to
the network and do something,

00:32:59.932 --> 00:33:01.390
that's not going
to be a good idea.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:02.848
It's going to turn
out pretty slow.

00:33:07.275 --> 00:33:09.830
It's not possible to put
everything in memory.

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:14.340
So if you're trying to
process two terabytes,

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:15.700
don't try to put in memory.

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:17.800
That's something
that maybe the data

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:20.240
scientists coming from a
completely different background

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:20.760
were doing.

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:24.300
And for us it was a challenge to
adopt the whole thing in a more

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:27.220
distributed way.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:30.130
And Dataflow has
PCollections that

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:34.310
really serve for that aspect.

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:39.100
And another thing that you need
to be aware of is the hot keys.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:43.130
The hot keys come from
a very common operation

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:46.930
you do with Dataflow which is,
for example, group by key where

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:48.110
you have a key.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:54.210
And that key might have
several actual values

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:57.140
matching with their key,
like millions of them.

00:33:57.140 --> 00:34:05.900
So if you see your graph and
you see your Compute instance

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:10.900
running, you see 203
might have very high CPU.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:13.040
And the others are idle.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:14.685
That means you have a hot key.

00:34:14.685 --> 00:34:16.060
And there are
solutions for that.

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:18.604
I'm going to touch
about that later.

00:34:22.564 --> 00:34:24.480
A good thing when you're
working with Dataflow

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:27.139
is to take advantage
of the local runner.

00:34:27.139 --> 00:34:31.110
So Dataflow has several runners.

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:32.929
When you start
working with-- when

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:34.810
we're developing this
model, we of course

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:40.050
started with small data
sets and writing our program

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:43.199
and testing it and going
through small iterations.

00:34:43.199 --> 00:34:45.350
And then you can actually
move to GCP runner.

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:53.239
So this is a good way to
deal with the hot keys.

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:55.920
So for example, when you need to
do something like aggregations

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:59.220
over these large
set of data, you

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:01.970
can actually use the
combine functions.

00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:07.940
So the combine functions allow
you to basically distribute

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:13.290
these values that you have for
several keys into worker nodes.

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:20.750
And it shows Dataflow the
semantics of your program

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:22.570
that you're trying to implement.

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:23.900
So they have several steps.

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:26.660
You basically initialize
the accumulators.

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:28.840
You define accumulators
to where you

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:31.730
will be doing something
like, for example, summing

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:33.630
the values.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:35.450
And then you need to
have a function that

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:39.630
will add each element
to the accumulators.

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:42.350
And then of course
you need to merge.

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:43.980
And then you might merge again.

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:48.000
And then you extract the output.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:49.430
And this is how
it looks in code.

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:54.430
You [INAUDIBLE] from
the combine function

00:35:54.430 --> 00:36:02.130
and specific inputs, accumulator
type and output type.

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:08.310
And you got there the initialize
function, the add input,

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:13.590
merge accumulators,
and extract outputs.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:15.480
And when you want
to use it, just

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.730
basically use the combine
perKey and create an instance

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:23.610
of your combine function.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:25.850
The composite transforms--
this is another one

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:28.410
that we found really useful.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.860
So as I was telling you,
this model is pretty complex,

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:32.880
several aggregations,
several levels,

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:34.160
joins and stuff like that.

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:39.630
Once we actually finish
implementing the whole thing,

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:42.510
it was a big mess, lot
of notes connecting

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:44.990
each other's, company crazy.

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:46.540
So composite
transforms allow you

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:48.930
to group related operations.

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:51.290
And you can have something
much easier to digest.

00:36:51.290 --> 00:36:56.960
So the way to implement is you
extend this PTransform class

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:59.130
and specify an input
type, output type.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:05.030
And then you chain the
two transforms together.

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:08.700
So for example
here, we are having

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:12.940
an initially transform that we
put in this case a PCollection.

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:16.770
And then we apply a second
transform on top of it.

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:19.300
And we return the results.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:23.360
And we went for
something like that.

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:28.030
And this is just a small
portion of the whole big model

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:31.560
to just one [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:37.240
And you can see how
much simpler it gets.

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:40.740
And again, I want to
reiterate, the team

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:43.930
was just two
engineers, just the two

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:46.150
of us trying to deal
with this big problem.

00:37:48.660 --> 00:37:52.370
So at the moment,
what we're doing now

00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:57.460
is that we're trying to
move from a batch mode,

00:37:57.460 --> 00:38:02.150
because the model was basically
conceived in a batch mode.

00:38:02.150 --> 00:38:05.190
The natural evolution, we
would be in a streaming mode.

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:08.420
So our data scientists
have taken more data

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:11.550
and they are running their
simulations and stuff

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:14.690
like that so we can actually
move to the next stage.

00:38:17.898 --> 00:38:23.390
So just to wrap up,
Telstra was having

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.387
a lot of issues trying to
analyze all this amount of data

00:38:26.387 --> 00:38:26.970
that they had.

00:38:29.950 --> 00:38:32.170
By using BigQuery,
we were finally

00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:35.300
able to allow them to
extract useful insights out

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:36.875
of all these amount of data.

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:44.990
When we were given this
very complex model,

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:48.190
Dataflow allows to actually
implement it in a way

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:51.830
that it could be maintainable
and we could iterate on it.

00:38:54.690 --> 00:39:02.450
And using GCP gave us access
to a very powerful and scalable

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:06.120
infrastructure at a fraction
of the cost compared

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:09.774
to actually rolling out our
own infrastructure or renting

00:39:09.774 --> 00:39:10.940
infrastructure from Telstra.

00:39:14.049 --> 00:39:15.590
And we didn't really
need a big team.

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:19.230
So that's all.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:19.810
Thank you.

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:23.160
[APPLAUSE]

